WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:16.377
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Case Study Analysis. Today you will evaluate past business decisions with should + perfect.

4
00:00:16.677 --> 00:00:19.341
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.602
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.428
Case study.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:30.262
Case study means a detailed analysis of a real situation.

8
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.593
The case study examined a failed coffee chain.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:37.103
Your turn. Say: Case study.

10
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:40.968
Case study.

11
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:42.544
Problem.

12
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.385
Problem means a difficulty that needs to be solved.

13
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:49.691
Define the problem before suggesting solutions.

14
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.634
Your turn. Say: Problem.

15
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.211
Problem.

16
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.137
Analysis.

17
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.903
Analysis means detailed examination of the elements of something.

18
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:06.503
Her analysis identified three root causes.

19
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:09.595
Your turn. Say: Analysis.

20
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:13.597
Analysis.

21
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:15.351
Option.

22
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.651
Option means a possible choice.

23
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.633
We compared three options for expansion.

24
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:25.066
Your turn. Say: Option.

25
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:28.750
Option.

26
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:30.912
Recommendation.

27
00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:35.402
Recommendation means a suggestion about the best course of action.

28
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:38.388
The report ends with a clear recommendation.

29
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:41.392
Your turn. Say: Recommendation.

30
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:45.792
Recommendation.

31
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.609
Evidence.

32
00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:51.708
Evidence means facts or data that support a conclusion.

33
00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:54.885
Every claim must be supported by evidence.

34
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:58.205
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

35
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:02.056
Evidence.

36
00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:04.131
Implementation.

37
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:08.707
Implementation means the process of putting a plan into action.

38
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:11.537
Implementation will take six months.

39
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.081
Your turn. Say: Implementation.

40
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:19.329
Implementation.

41
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.061
Outcome.

42
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.038
Outcome means the final result.

43
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.998
The outcome exceeded expectations.

44
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:30.120
Your turn. Say: Outcome.

45
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:33.979
Outcome.

46
00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:38.452
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:46.868
Visal, in this case study, a Cambodian juice company expanded to forty outlets and went bankrupt within two years. What went wrong?

48
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:54.815
The evidence suggests they grew too fast. They should have tested the model in five outlets before opening forty.

49
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:57.475
Was the product the problem?

50
00:02:58.583 --> 00:03:08.309
Not really. Sales per outlet were strong at first. However, they shouldn't have signed long, expensive leases in tourist areas.

51
00:03:09.417 --> 00:03:11.915
So what would you recommend for similar firms?

52
00:03:13.017 --> 00:03:22.356
Expand gradually, monitor cash flow weekly and prepare an exit option for each location. They should have planned for a downturn.

53
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.931
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:28.944
Grammar focus: Should plus perfect for evaluation.

55
00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:30.338
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.398
The company should have entered the market earlier.

57
00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:36.318
They should have tested the model first.

58
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:40.377
The managers shouldn't have signed such expensive leases.

59
00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:54.623
'Should have + past participle' criticises or regrets a past decision; 'shouldn't have + p.p.' says a past action was a mistake. Compare: 'could have' (a possibility that didn't happen) and 'must have' (a logical deduction).

60
00:03:55.383 --> 00:03:59.057
Be careful! Don't say: They should entered earlier.

61
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.476
Say: They should have entered earlier.

62
00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:05.903
Should have + past participle.

63
00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.248
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:11.603
Thinking Like a Consultant.

65
00:04:11.903 --> 00:04:18.175
A business case study presents a real situation and asks the reader to diagnose problems and recommend solutions.

66
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:26.913
Strong analysis begins by separating symptoms, such as falling sales, from root causes, such as poor positioning or weak cash management.

67
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:35.565
Analysts then generate several options and evaluate each against evidence, cost and risk rather than choosing the first attractive idea.

68
00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:44.267
Looking back, it is easy to say what a company should have done, so good analysis also asks what managers could reasonably have known at the time.

69
00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:52.584
Finally, every recommendation needs a realistic implementation plan, because even the best strategy fails without execution.

70
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.922
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.783 --> 00:05:00.853
They blank the contract more carefully.

72
00:05:01.153 --> 00:05:05.363
A, should read. B, should have read. C, should had read.

73
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.876
The answer is B: should have read.

74
00:05:12.017 --> 00:05:14.968
A suggestion about the best course of action is a blank.

75
00:05:15.268 --> 00:05:19.316
A, recommendation. B, problem. C, case study.

76
00:05:22.867 --> 00:05:24.924
The answer is A: recommendation.

77
00:05:26.083 --> 00:05:28.988
The process of putting a plan into action is blank.

78
00:05:29.288 --> 00:05:33.070
A, evidence. B, outcome. C, implementation.

79
00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:39.213
The answer is C: implementation.

80
00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.117
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:42.417 --> 00:05:48.671
A, They shouldn't have spent so much.. B, They shouldn't had spent so much.. C, They shouldn't have spend so much..

82
00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:55.405
The answer is A: They shouldn't have spent so much..

83
00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.419
According to the reading, strong analysis separates symptoms from…

84
00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:04.674
A, root causes. B, customers. C, profits.

85
00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:10.140
The answer is A: root causes.

86
00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:14.427
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.283 --> 00:06:18.746
What is the most important step in analysing a business case?

88
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.224
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.524 --> 00:07:12.275
In my view, the most important step is defining the real problem correctly. Many students jump to solutions, such as cutting prices, when falling sales are only a symptom of a deeper cause like poor positioning or weak management. Once the root cause is clear, the analyst can compare options using evidence. For example, a company that blamed competitors should have examined its own customer service first. A precise diagnosis makes every later recommendation stronger and more realistic.

90
00:07:12.575 --> 00:07:14.238
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.008
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and should plus perfect for evaluation.

92
00:07:20.308 --> 00:07:25.074
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:07:25.374 --> 00:07:31.245
Congratulations! You have finished Online C one, Book four. See you in Online C two!

94
00:07:31.545 --> 00:07:34.359
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

